WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:12.690
This video is about the de Broglie hypothesis
which supposedly says matter is a wave as

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:17.560
well as a particle and this equation that
tells us how to find the ‘wavelength’

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.310
of a particle.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:24.320
The issue is, there is a very precise way
to interpret the hypothesis that leads to

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.489
interesting questions about quantum mechanics,
but instead it’s often stated in an almost

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:31.750
meaninglessly imprecise form.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.899
Let’s fix that.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:37.970
This video is a bit more involved then the
previous ones but hopefully not too bad!

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:42.829
But if I fail, don’t worry, you don’t
need to understand all of this video to get

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:44.170
the next.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.719
If you do want to stick around.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:52.210
I recommend a little revision since it’s
been a while–in particular these videos.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.210
Ok, we’ve got this particle, it’s got
a wavefunction.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.739
Remember the wavefunction of the particle
contains everything we can know about that

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:01.739
particle.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:06.229
For example, it might say that the particle
is in a superposition of being here, here

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:12.710
and here, and so if you measure it’s position
then it will turn up in one of those places.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.840
There are these special wavefunctions called
position eigenstates.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.659
This means that the particle is really only
in one spot- if you measure it, it will definitely

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:24.170
be there.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.770
Wavefunctions doesn’t just tell us about
the position of a particle, it also tells

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.280
us about the speed, or momentum, of the particle.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.439
There is a way to convert a wavefunction from
the position basis to the momentum one.

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.210
When you do it you could find your particle
is in a superposition of several speeds.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:48.100
Here’s the question we’ll focus on: if
the particle is going at one speed, so it’s

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.909
wavefunction is a momentum eigenstate, then
where is it?

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.330
How do we write that wavefunction is the position
basis?

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:01.049
Is it in a superposition of many places, or
is it in one spot?

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:06.299
If this was every day life, at any point in
time a moving particle is still only in one

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:07.409
spot.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:12.890
But what we’ll see is, even at one particular
time, a momentum eigenstate is a superposition

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:17.870
of many positions- in fact, all of them, equally.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.970
I’ll show how we motivate this.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:26.810
As with Noether’s theorem, we do thought
experiments about how a state could be different.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.750
We say, suppose we have some superposition
of locations.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.540
We just imagine, what if these places had
been a bit to the right instead?

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.560
Let’s call this offset amount delta.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.600
As you can see, position wavefunctions are
the same but delta to the right.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.860
Remember the way we defined this shift, the
position is changed, but the speeds the particle

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.430
is going at is not.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:57.110
Ok so imagine our original wavefunction was
a momentum eigenstate, what happens to it

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.610
when it’s shifted?

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:02.340
We’d expect this wavefunction is exactly
the same right?

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.980
Because the shift doesn’t affect the speed,
so it should still be the same momentum wavefunction.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.790
Well actually, that doesn’t mean the wavefunction
has to be exactly the same.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.180
We definitely want it to be going at the same
speed if we measure it.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.610
But there’s still some freedom here.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:26.120
Remember the probability of a particular speed
is the length of the coefficient in front

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.349
here.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:32.220
Imagine if the state had a negative one in
front of it.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.390
Negative one still has length one.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.200
That’s means the probability of getting
this speed is still 1.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:45.250
What about if there was an i here That still
has length 1.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.660
In fact, if we go back to thinking of complex
numbers as arrows, all complex number on this

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.090
circle has length one.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.500
In physics we call these numbers ‘phases’.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.459
There’s really convenient way to represent
a phases.

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:02.299
Suppose this phase has angle theta.

00:04:02.299 --> 00:04:06.599
Using our trigonometry, we could write that
as this complex number.

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:23.120
So we’re just going to define this expression
to be a complex exponential.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.770
Multiplying an complex number by a phase is
the same as just rotating it by that angle.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.270
That’ll be useful.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.610
Getting back to the point,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:38.300
we require that, when you shift a momentum
eigenstate in position, it doesn’t change

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.990
momentum, but it may pick up a phase.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:46.001
This phase obviously needs to depend on the
amount of shifting delta and it also depends

00:04:46.001 --> 00:04:53.070
on the momentum, so those two things should
go into the angle, but then we need a number

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:58.020
that scales how much that the angle changes
as you change delta and p.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.220
We’re going to call it oh I don’t know,
how about... h bar.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:07.949
Yes the same constant in all those famous
quantum mechanics equations.

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:13.460
This is it’s grand entry- it’s just a
constant there that decides how much a shift

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.250
changes the phase.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.700
We can measure it some other ways, and it
turns out it is very very small.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.050
We’re almost there.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.690
But first we’ve got to talk a little bit
more about position wavefunctions.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.200
I’ve talked about about the wavefunction
when it’s a superposition of a few different

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.200
spots.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:37.680
But the thing is, a particle could be in superposition
of a whole infinite range of positions.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.960
What if the particle is in a superposition
of all these places but it’s more likely

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.570
to turn up in this area?

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.770
How do we represent that?

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:51.760
Before we put a complex number in front of
each position.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.419
Now we’re basically going to do the same,
we’ll assign a complex number to each point

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:03.310
the particle could be, in other words along
the whole line.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.530
Assigning a complex number to every point
on the line- that’s just a complex function

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.039
-we’ll call it f(x).

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:17.720
The bigger the complex number, the more likely
the particle will turn up around there.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.289
There are two ways we can draw this complex
function, either, we just draw the real and

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:27.930
imaginary part separately, or draw it 3D,
with the real bit coming out toward you, and

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:32.260
the imaginary bit going up, so all complex
numbers are just arrows.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:34.430
Both are useful.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:38.889
For people who’ve seen calculus before,
all I’m saying is that the wavefunction

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:46.419
can be written as an integral of all positions,
where each position is weighted by this complex

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:47.800
function.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.389
If that made no sense to you, don’t worry,
it’s not important.

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:57.800
Anyway, we have this momentum eigenstate,
and we want to write it in the position basis.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:03.160
We know the position wavefunction is going
to be represented one of these complex functions,

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.550
we just need to find it.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:11.110
Remember that when you translate the momentum
eigenstate, it stays the same, except it picks

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.470
up an overall phase.

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:21.069
Then since this function is the momentum eigenstate,
it must do the same.

00:07:21.069 --> 00:07:26.520
Well there is a function that fits that bill:
it is this complex squiggly function: where

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.599
the blue is the real bit and the red is the
imaginary.

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:36.250
In other words this function is a complex
exponential with this wavelength.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.090
Why is this the function we’re looking for?

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:45.009
Well you can already see it almost has translation
symmetry, the function is the same if you

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:46.819
shift it over a bit...

00:07:46.819 --> 00:07:49.020
only it’s a bit rotated.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.820
But that’s exactly what would have happened
if we multiplied by a phase!

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:58.020
Remember, multiplying any complex number by
a phase just rotates it by that angle.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:04.169
So translating is this function is the same
as just multiplying it by a phase.

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:07.919
We’ve now actually derived the de Broglie
hypothesis!

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:16.020
See, all it actually says is, the wavefunction
of a particle with precise momentum is the

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.750
complex exponential with this wavelength.

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:24.520
Why the confusion then about the wave particle
duality, where people say a particle acts

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.240
like a wave sometimes and like a particle
sometimes.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:33.370
I think the problem is that for a confusing
time before quantum mechanics, people where

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:38.610
doing things like the double slit experiment
and for understandable reasons, interpreted

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:43.290
them using the wave/ particle duality.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:49.250
Let me show you an alternative explanation
using all of our current quantum mechnaics.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:54.400
The way the double or single slit experiment
is done is by firing particles, let’s say

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.400
atoms.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:00.030
Those atoms are set up to have one precise
speed.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:04.420
In other word’s each atom’s wavefunction
is a momentum eigenstate.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:09.730
We’ll just look at the real bit, and so
that looks a wave.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:14.870
If we have a single slit, that wave, after
it passes through the slit, spreads out- just

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:16.500
like a water wave would.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.190
Then we measure where the particles end up.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:22.700
As you can see, the wavefunction is biggest
in the middle, and so that’s where each

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.210
particle is most likely to land.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:30.510
But that’s what we would have expected even
if we were throwing tennis balls you know?

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:34.720
So people thought, this is purely particle
like behaviour.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.530
The double slit experiment then should be
similar- each particle goes through one slit

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:43.800
and lands most likely right behind that slit.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.580
Instead they got this.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:48.780
This is how you explain it with quantum mechanics.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:52.800
Since we don’t measure which door the particle
goes through, each particle’s wavefunction

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.290
is a superposition of going through both doors.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.150
This means we add the separate wavefunctions
together.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:00.280
But interesting things happen when you add
waves.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.610
If the waves were in sync, like they are at
these points because the crests meet other

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:09.930
crests and the toughs meet other troughs here,
the result is a really big wave.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:14.920
But here, the waves are totally out of sync,
and so there’s no wave left there at all-

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.280
they cancel.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:21.140
The result is that there are these alternating
spots were the wavefunction is big then small

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:26.770
then big again etc etc, to get the right result.

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:31.300
People thought now the particle is acting
as a wave, but wasn’t before.

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:37.170
But as you can see the current interpretation
is much more subtle, but covers both cases

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.490
in the same framework.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:45.000
People also imagined that the particle itself
smeared out into a physical wave, but collected

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:48.960
itself back up as a point particle when measured
at the wall.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.710
Now we say that the particle itself is doing
who knows what while it’s not measured,

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:57.850
if it exists at all, but it’s wavefunction
is the one that spreads out.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:02.511
Honestly, I’m not sure why people who know
quantum mechanics still talk about the wave/

00:11:02.511 --> 00:11:08.390
particle duality at all- maybe because that
seems easier to explain than the wavefunction?

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:10.810
But it annoys me because it makes the world
sound so

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:13.840
paradoxical so we couldn’t possibly hope
to comprehend it.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:15.460
In the end, maybe that’s the case, but we
won’t know until we try to understand quantum

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:16.460
mechanics.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:20.350
Anyway, thanks for watching this video- I
know I’ve been away for far far too long.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.020
As a reward for your loyalty, here’s some
homework.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:29.460
First, What did you think of my supposed derivation
of the momentum wavefunction?

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:33.010
What assumptions did I make that you think
need more justification (there are a few keys

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:36.910
one I saw)
Second, Nils Bohr was a huge proponent of

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:43.940
the wave/particle duality even long after
the modern form of quantum mechanics.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:48.090
Try and understand his argument for it and
report back.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:50.970
Ok, see you very soon for the Heisenberg uncertainty
principle.

